  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: write detailed tutorial implementing bounded LRU cache in Python using collections.OrderedDict. Include complete class, explain get and put behavior, edge cases, tests, worked example, complexity analysis. Precise, meaningful prose and code.\n\nNeed produce final answer only. Need plan content.\n\nWe should write a tutorial. Need likely include code: LRUCache class with capacity, OrderedDict. Methods get(key), put(key,value). Also maybe contains? __contains__, __getitem__, __setitem__, clear, size, maybe remove. Edge cases: capacity <=0 behavior? Should we decide. Usually capacity positive. But tutorial discuss if capacity <=0: either raise ValueError or no caching. Need choose. For robust: validate capacity >0 in __init__, else raise ValueError. Or support nonpositive by not storing. But bounded LRU typically capacity positive. Mention alternative. Let's implement raising ValueError. But if user expects capacity 0? We can discuss edge case and maybe provide option strict? Let's be precise.\n\nUse OrderedDict: move_to_end(key) on get to mark most recently used; on put existing key update and move to end; if full, popitem(last=False) to remove least recently used.\n\nComplete class:\n\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        if capacity <= 0: raise ValueError...\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self._cache = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key, default=None):\n        if key not in self._cache: return default\n        value = self._cache.pop(key) ? If using move_to_end simpler: self._cache.move_to_end(key); return self._cache[key]\nBut need careful: doing key in then move. Could use try/except KeyError for atomic? In Python no concurrency by default. For tutorial mention no locking.\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if key in self._cache:\n            self._cache[key]=value\n            self._cache.move_to_end(key)\n        else:\n            if len(self._cache) >= self.capacity:\n                self._cache.popitem(last=False)\n            self._cache[key]=value\n\n    def __contains__ etc.\n\nMaybe include peek(key) not recency? Could be nice. But user asked complete class, get and put behavior. We can include additional methods for completeness but not overdo.\n\nTests: use",
